{"question_id": "qst_0327", "question_type": "intra_document_reasoning", "source_types": ["confluence"], "question": "In the Unified Inference Consumption Paradigm overview, what are the two unit primitives used for billing, and where is the referenced Confluence file that contains the pricing catalog and token definitions for them?", "expected_doc_ids": ["dsid_69e745b76aa34eab8721ba0c88482ec0"], "gold_answer": "The two billing unit primitives are the token-generation-unit (TGU) and the embedding-unit (EU). The pricing catalog and token definitions for them are referenced in the Confluence file at \"/confluence/product-docs/pricing-and-packaging/token-bundle-flex-enterprise-metering-2026-08-20.json\".", "answer_facts": ["The two billing unit primitives are the token-generation-unit (TGU) and the embedding-unit (EU).", "The pricing catalog and token definitions for these units are referenced in the Confluence file at /confluence/product-docs/pricing-and-packaging/token-bundle-flex-enterprise-metering-2026-08-20.json."]}
{'question_id': 'qst_0178', 'question_type': 'semantic', 'source_types': ['github'], 'question': 'In the recent change that made low bit math safer for inference, what is the default pass rate used to decide when a machine is allowed to step down from the safest numeric mode?', 'expected_doc_ids': ['dsid_22a7e9af2760474dbdf611c2c21cd231'], 'gold_answer': 'The default pass-rate threshold is 0.92 (i.e., 92% of the microbenchmark runs must pass) before a node is allowed to step down from the safest precision.', 'answer_facts': ['The default pass-rate threshold is 0.92.', 'A node is allowed to step down from the safest precision only if at least 92% of the microbenchmark runs pass.']}
{'question_id': 'qst_0180', 'question_type': 'semantic', 'source_types': ['github'], 'question': 'In our rollout system, what is the name of the new mechanism that prevents a candidate release from getting full user traffic until a dry run with replayed requests and smoke checks has passed?', 'expected_doc_ids': ['dsid_730ed8ee5fc84e0b9be761003b9a60d3'], 'gold_answer': "It's the **TrafficEscrow** (the **traffic_escrow** service), which escrows routing/commit promotion and only releases it after a rehearse run with replayed requests and smoke checks succeeds.", 'answer_facts': ['The new mechanism is named TrafficEscrow.', 'TrafficEscrow is implemented as the traffic_escrow service.', 'TrafficEscrow prevents a candidate release from getting full user traffic by escrowing routing or commit promotion until a rehearse dry run with replayed requests and smoke checks succeeds.']}
{'question_id': 'qst_0189', 'question_type': 'semantic', 'source_types': ['github'], 'question': 'In our offline installer release process, how do we expose per-component supply chain provenance so an operator can see which build system and commit produced each piece and optionally enforce that missing or bad attestations fail the index step?', 'expected_doc_ids': ['dsid_5d54f5f89bbc48a5b06b8dead39729b3'], 'gold_answer': 'We extend the private-installer release index (schema v2) to include an per-artifact `origins` block that records the source repo, build system/job ID, build commit, and optional SBOM/cosign references, populated by the indexer querying an origin-mapper service and cross-referencing artifact digests to upstream build metadata. The indexer verifies cosign signatures on the origin blobs and records the verification result, and operators can make failed/missing origin verification a hard failure by running the indexer with `--enforce-origin-verification`.', 'answer_facts': ['The private-installer release index schema v2 is extended to include a per-artifact origins block.', 'Each per-artifact origins block records the source repository, build system or job ID, and build commit that produced the artifact, and can also include optional SBOM and cosign references.', 'The indexer populates the origins block by querying an origin-mapper service and cross-referencing artifact digests to upstream build metadata.', 'The indexer verifies cosign signatures on the origin blobs and records the verification result in the index.', 'Operators can make missing or failed origin verification a hard failure by running the indexer with --enforce-origin-verification.']}
